\section{Introduction}
Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across diverse domains, such as literary and professional writing, code generation, and problems requiring logical reasoning. However, their performance towards a specific task remains heavily dependent on the quality of instructions — or prompts — provided to them \cite{fernando2023promptbreeder, guo2025evopromptconnectingllmsevolutionary}. The term \textit{prompt engineering} has become widely used, signaling that prompting has become an important skill for harnessing these models' full potential. This skill requires specialized expertise attained through learning techniques and significant trial-and-error. Furthermore, when prompts prove insufficient for a task, developers must either implement dedicated post-processing logic or employ fine-tuning strategies to address performance gaps. In that sense, the development of systems executing complex tasks while solely relying on prompt engineering is resource intensive, thus motivating the need for an automated method to optimize prompts.
%users engage in manually editing and applying post-processing to generated outputs as a means to compensate for poor performance. In that sense, the devElopment of systems capable of executing tasks through effective prompt engineering is extremely costly (in terms of time and resources), thus motivating a need for an automated method to optimize prompts, that reduce these associated costs.
%In that sense, effective prompting has emerged as a critical bottleneck, which strongly motivates its automation and optimization.% 

