The automated optimization of prompts is especially challenging for tasks where performance or quality is judged subjectively, where ambiguity challenges require resolution, or where managing conflicting contexts is paramount \cite{suzgun2022challenging}. In these scenarios, agents are expected to learn to adapt synthesizing different types of information, make judgment calls from different perspectives, and self-ascertain branching and stopping conditions during its iteration process; all in the absence of any quality criteria or scoring functions to quantify success along said criteria.  

% Optimization of prompts is additionally challenging with tasks on which performance or quality is judged subjectively, such as long form creative writing or execution of highly specialized expert professional work. Think writing a good novel or strong and compelling legal brief, as opposed to solving a math problem or coding a function. For these, judging the success and quality of task completion can simultaneously involve many hard to define criteria. These strongly resist characterization with numeric scores or well-defined fitness functions over which to optimize. Simply put, for some tasks, basic questions such as "Is this good enough to stop here?" and "How much improvement did we get on the last step?" become hard to answer.

Current approaches to automated discrete prompt optimization fall into two primary categories: gradient-based methods and evolutionary strategies. Gradient-based methods operate on textual gradients defined by means of LLM generated critical feedback (examples of these are Protegi \cite{pryzant2023automaticpromptoptimizationgradient} and TextGrad \cite{yuksekgonul2024textgradautomaticdifferentiationtext}). These methods offer computational efficiency but typically require labeled ground truth data from which to calculate loss; risk task-specific overfitting especially when there is a lack of diversity in the examples; and do not have a mechanism to perform exploration and thus suffer from adaptability issues. Conversely, evolutionary methods (such as EvoPrompt \cite{guo2025evopromptconnectingllmsevolutionary} and PromptBreeder {\cite{fernando2023promptbreeder}}) provide broader exploration capabilities but suffer from computational inefficiency due to random search and, crucially, depend on well-defined objective fitness functions — which are often unavailable for subjective tasks.

We introduce DEEVO (DEbate-driven EVOlutionary prompt optimization), a novel framework that addresses the challenges of 1) exploration vs exploitation, and 2) lack of labeled ground truth / fitness functions, by guiding prompt evolution through structured debates and Elo-based selection.

\textbf{Exploration vs Exploitation}
\newline Unlike previous approaches, DEEVO enables systematic exploration of the prompt space through two innovative evolutionary mechanisms. At its core, DEEVO employs multi-agent debate \cite{du2023improving} to guide intelligent crossover, where strengths and weaknesses of parent prompts are identified before strategically combining their effective elements. This process is complemented by targeted prompt mutations that specifically modify instructions to improve task performance. DEEVO's evolutionary approach selectively incorporates elements from both successful and unsuccessful prompts based on their identified strengths, preserving prompt effectiveness and logical structure while systematically exploring the solution space.

% Unlike previous approaches, DEEVO enables exploration of the prompt space via running the processes of intelligent crossover that identify strengths and weaknesses of parent prompts before appropriately combining them; targeted prompt mutations that modify prompt instructions to improve clarity and flow, and splicing and recombination that cross-pollinate prompts based on multi-agent debate \cite{du2023improving} of their respective outputs. DEEVO's evolutionary process selectively incorporates elements from both successful and unsuccessful prompts based on their identified strengths from the debate process. This approach maintains the integrity, coherence, and consistency of complex instructions while facilitating meaningful exploration of the prompt design space.

\textbf{Lack of Labeled Ground-Truth Data}
\newline Unlike prior prompt optimization strategies, DEEVO does not rely on labeled data / fitness functions against ground truth in order to evaluate prompt effectiveness. Instead, it leverages LLM-powered multi-agent debates (MAD) \cite{du2023improving} to evaluate prompt quality without requiring predetermined metrics. By having LLMs critique prompt outputs in a pairwise fashion and determine a winner through structured debates, DEEVO ensures a self-contained evaluation system that can assess quality across diverse tasks — including those with subjective criteria. The evaluation mechanism evolves and improves along with the prompts, incorporating novel ideas as candidate criteria for future evaluation. The resulting debate verdicts serve as a fitness proxy that simultaneously drives improvement and preserves valuable diversity in the prompt population without needing a manually crafted or separately learned objective function for fitness selection.

\textbf{A Modified Elo-Based Selection}
\newline The Elo rating system \cite{elo1978rating} is a robust method to rank entities (in this case, prompts) via pairwise comparisons, where each prompt maintains a numerical rating that dynamically updates based on competition outcomes. This approach has gained significant traction in LLM evaluation frameworks, with numerous benchmark systems adopting Elo-based mechanisms to rank model and prompt performance \cite{ameli2024statisticalframeworkrankingllmbased, chiang2024chatbotarenaopenplatform, boubdir2023elouncoveredrobustnessbest}. Despite its robustness, Elo is particularly limited in its ability to handle newcomer prompt competitors (due to requiring many matchups to reach an accurate skill assessment); and veteran prompt competitors (due to their ratings becoming "sticky" over time, as historical matchups dilute recent performances). For this reason, DEEVO utilizes a modified Elo-selection mechanism that introduces selection quotas for newcomers and veterans; to force the prompt population to always consist of a balanced proportion of newcomer and veteran prompts. This achieves faster calibration for new prompt candidates, better capture current skill levels for veterans, and provide more accurate performance estimates. 

