\begin{abstract}
The topology of a power grid is estimated using an information theoretic approach. By modeling the grid as a graph and using voltage magnitude data of individual nodes in the grid, the mutual information between pairs of nodes is computed using different approximation methods. Using the well-known Chow-Liu algorithm, a maximum spanning tree based on mutual information is computed to estimate the power grid topology. Experiments and results are presented to optimize this approach with success shown for IEEE networks generated with MATPOWER and data generated using GridLAB-D. The algorithm is then cross-validated on IEEE networks generated by the European Union Joint Research Council. 
\end{abstract}






%% main text
\section{Motivation}
\label{S:1}  

New distributed energy resources (DERs) are gaining widespread use in the electrical grids of many developed and less-economically developed countries. Solar and wind power plants cause daily reconfiguration in the electrical grid topology, controlled often by a vast array of digitally controlled switches. For the ever-changing topology of the grid, options to save energy, reduce costs and increase load stability are all very important tasks. The true topology of the electrical grid is often a useful tool in many simulations and control algorithms to optimize power transmission~\cite{liao2018urban}. 

The grid utility companies often possess outdated or mislabeled electrical grid maps~\cite{park2020learning}. An untrue map impairs the ability of the utility company to observe power flow and optimize power flow. For instance, perhaps the utility company is deciding where to place a massive 500 kWh flow-battery to improve load stability. Knowing the topology of the grid can help the utility company optimize placement of the battery. Often the utility companies will have a partial topology of the electrical grid which has been confirmed~\cite{lampe2013power}, and they wish to use this partial topology to confirm whether outlying sub-sections of the grid are correctly mapped. 

This work seeks to answer several questions.

\begin{enumerate}

\item{Can the Chow-Liu algorithm \cite{chow1968approximating, koller2009probabilistic} be used to correctly estimate grid topology based on voltage magnitude measurements?} 
\item{Can the Chow-Liu algorithm estimate sub-sections of the grid accurately?} 
\item{What are the limiting factors to successful performance of the Chow-Liu algorithm with realistic grid data.} 
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}

Previously, researchers in Ref.~\cite{liao2015distribution, liao2016urban} have defined the problem statement for grid estimation using smart-meter data, we provide a summary here. We can define a distribution grid as a physical network with branches that connect different buses. To utilize the time series data collected by smart meters, for a $M$ bus system, we construct a graphical model $ G = (S,E)$ where $ S = \{1,2,...M\}$ is the set of vertices, and $ E = \{e_{ik},i,k \in S\} $ represents the set of the unidirectional edges meaning $ e_{ik} = e_{ki}$. In this model, a node corresponds to a bus in the physical layer and is modeled as a random variable $V_{i}$. The edge that connects node $i$ and $k$ represents the statistical dependence measured between bus $i$ and $k$. If we assume for a noiseless system that measurements are taken in steady state and all voltages are sinusoidal signals at the same frequency, then the voltage measurement at bus $i$ is defined as $ v_{i}[t] = \lvert v_{i}[t] \rvert e^{(j\delta_{i}[t])}$, where $\lvert v_{i}[t] \rvert$ is the voltage magnitude of node $i$ at time $t$ and $\delta_{i}[t]$ is the voltage phase at node $i$ at time $t$.

We seek to reconstruct the grid bus topology using voltage magnitude data (and phase data when available). Given a sequence of of historical measurements $\lvert v_{i}(t)\rvert$ (and in some cases $\delta_{i}[t]$ as well) we seek to find the grid topology.

\subsection{Previous Work}

The Stanford Sustainable Systems Lab (S3L) has done extensive work on grid estimation. The lab showed successful estimation performance with the Chow-Liu algorithm using a Gaussian approximation to the voltage distribution of individual nodes to calculate the mutual information between nodes~\cite{weng2016distributed}. The data used in this article was generated using MATPOWER~\cite{zimmerman2010matpower} a well-known grid simulation package for Matlab. The work described in this document seeks to reproduce the results of the Rajagopal group for an IEEE 123-bus network data generated with MATPOWER and extend results to data generated with GridLAB-D~\cite{chassin2008gridlab}, a software package developed by the Pacific Northwest National Laboratory to simulate power flow and control of electrical grids. 

\subsection{Smart Meter Data}

Using voltage data at different buses, our task is to estimate the connectivity of a sub-section or in some cases the entire topology of the electrical grid. For the scope of this document we focus on analyzing data sets where voltage magnitude data are available but voltage phase data are not. This document analyzes data that have been generated with two different software packages. The simulated data seek to represent data that could be gathered from smart-meters that take voltage measurements at constant time intervals. Data have been generated using MATPOWER to create IEEE standard networks, which are labeled as IEEE data sets. Using GridLAB-D, data sets have been generated to simulate realistic power profiles. The GridLAB-D data sets in some cases also have solar power introduced into the grid. The GridLAB-D data sets are labeled SG (non-solar) data sets when solar is not present in the grid and SG (solar) data sets when solar power is present in the grid. The European Union (E.U.) Joint Research Council created several realistic network models to model power flow in European urban, semi-urban and rural environments~\cite{prettico2016distribution}. The algorithm is tested on these data sets which are referred to as European data-sets in this work. 

\subsection{Main Conclusions}

This work performs experiments on different data sets, different lengths of data, different down-sampling schemes and different levels of precision of data to visualize and quantify the performance of the grid estimation algorithm presented in this work. We find that the algorithm has more difficulty predicting larger (more nodes in the system) GridLAB-D data sets. Down-sampling GridLAB-D data sets severely reduces algorithm performance which is not the case for IEEE data sets. The length of data used for analysis is important up to a certain length before performance stabilizes (around 180 days worth of data for GridLAB-D data sets). Lastly, the quality of data is also important. We find that millivolts of voltage magnitude data and also that 8-bits worth of precision are important for optimal algorithm performance. 

The Algorithm was finally tested on European data sets from the E.U. Joint Research Council. The European data sets show mixed results where the Gaussian and JVHW mutual information methods perform on average vastly better than the discrete mutual information method. The SDR and leaf SDR do not differ greatly for when the change in voltage magnitude is used in place of the voltage magnitude unlike the GridLAB-D data sets. 

\section{Estimation Approach}
\label{S:2}

\subsection{Theoretical Approach}

We seek to find the grid topology from smart meter time series data of bus voltage measurements. We model the buses as random variables. We define bus 1 as the slack bus which is a constant with unity magnitude and zero phase angle. Therefore, we can use a joint probability distribution to represent the inter-dependency among buses. Note, since the slack bus is a constant, it is omitted from the joint probability equation below.

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:joint_probability}
P({\mathbf{V_{s}}}) = P(V_{2}, V_{3}, .... V_{M})
%% \textrm{  for  } 0 \leq j(i) \leq  i
\end{equation}

This equation can be further simplified using the chain rule.
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:joint_probability_chain}
P({\mathbf{V_{s}}}) = P(V_{2})P(V_{3}|V_{2}) .... P(V_{M}|V_{2},V_{3},...V_{M-1})
%% \textrm{  for  } 0 \leq j(i) \leq  i
\end{equation}

Voltage measurements usually have irregular probability distributions. Instead of using voltage measurements directly, we use the incremental change of the voltage measurement to reconstruct the distribution grid topology \cite{chen2016quickest}. A reasonable assumption is that the change in current at bus $i$ is independent given the change in current at neighboring nodes. Given this assumption, Dr. Liao and Prof. Weng have shown that the change in voltage distribution at a bus is conditionally independent to the change in voltage distributions of non-neighboring buses given the change in voltage distributions of neighboring buses \cite{liao2016urban}. In Figure \ref{fg:current_MI}, we see the mutual information of pairwise current variables. The relatively small mutual information means that one can approximate that the current are independent with some approximation error. 

\begin{figure}
\centering\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{current_MI.eps}
\caption{The pairwise mutual information of current phasor for the SG1 (non-solar) data set.}

\label{fg:current_MI}
\end{figure}
In a distribution grid, the correlation between interconnected neighboring buses is higher than that between non-neighbor buses. Therefore, with Theorem 1, the distribution of node i is independent from non-neighboring nodes: $ V_{i} \indep V_{j \notin N(i)} | V_{k\in N(i)}$ where $N(i)$ contains the vertices of the neighbors of bus i, i.e., $ N(i) = \{k \in S|e_{ik} \in E\} $.

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:first_order}
%P_{{V_{s}} = %\prod P_{i}(V_{i} | V_{(i)})
P({\mathbf{V_{S}}}) = \prod\limits_{i=2}^M P_{i}(V_{i} | V_{\mathbf{N(i)}}) 
%% \textrm{  for  } 0 \leq j(i) \leq  i
\end{equation}

Equation \ref{eq:first_order} simplifies the task of finding the connectivity of bus i to finding the neighboring nodes of bus i. 





