\subsection{Variable Change of Voltage Magnitude Step-size}
	Using the incremental change in voltage magnitude has shown very good performance with the IEEE and GridLAB-D data sets. We will see in later sections that the resolution of data (i.e. the time interval between samples) has a strong effect on the performance of the algorithm. We will show later that a resolution of 15 minutes can cause a noticeable drop in SDR with the SG1 and SG2 data sets. We find that using the change of voltage magnitude with step sizes larger than one minute also shows a similar drop in performance. The effect for a step-size of 2 minutes is shown below in Equation (\ref{eq:step_size}).

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:step_size}
\lvert \Delta V_{\text{Step Size 2}}[t] \rvert = \vert V[t] \rvert - \lvert V[t-2] \rvert 
\end{equation}
	We begin our analysis with the 34-node GridLAB-D data sets, SG1 and SG1 (solar). We can see in Figure \ref{fg:SG1_var_deriv} that both data sets show significant drop off in the SDR and leaf SDR for step sizes of 30 minutes or greater. The SDR drops off from around 100\% to less than 90\% for all MI methods for both SG1 data sets with 15 minute step-sizes. The discrete and JVHW methods estimate the SDR better than the Gaussian approximation as expected because the Gaussian approximation is a rather crude approximation for some nodes. The SDR plots show that change in voltage magnitude step sizes of greater than five minutes cause considerable degradation of estimation performance for the SG1 data sets. 

