\subsection{LV semi-urban data}

The first data set we analyze is the largest of the European data sets. 
The LV semi-urban data set has 114 nodes, with seven leaf nodes. The true graph can be seen in Figure \ref{fg:LV_semiurban_graph}. The network was modeled to have zero overhead power lines and to represent the power transmission network on the outskirts of a European city. 


\begin{figure}[h!]
\centering\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{LV_semiurban_graph.eps}
\caption{The the true graph of the LV semi-urban network. The graph contains 114 nodes. This image is courtesy of the Distribution System Operations Observatory E.U. Joint Research Council 2016 Report.}
\label{fg:LV_semiurban_graph}
\end{figure}

The voltage magnitude histogram at several nodes is shown in Figure~\ref{fg:LV_semiurban_hist}. The Gaussian approximation appears to be a poor fit for each node in the Figure. The voltage magnitude at each node is normalized relative to the feeder node. Node 1 is the node connected directly to the feeder.  Node 1 sees voltage magnitudes that are on average higher than the feeder node (the majority of values are above 1) suggesting there is a transformer in between Node 1 and the feeder to step up the voltage. The other sampled nodes show mean voltage magnitude values below one, i.e., below the mean feeder value and below the mean of Node 1. None of the profiles appear symmetric and suggest perhaps another distribution, for instance the gamma distribution, would better describe the voltage magnitude profiles in this network. This behavior suggests the nodes have voltage magnitudes largely concentrated in a small band, yet occasional dips in voltage magnitude are seen likely due to increased demand in the network. The Gaussian MI method performs poorly compared to the discrete and JVHW MI methods as seen in Table~\ref{tb:sdr_euro}. The Gaussian leaf SDR at 28.57\% performs very poorly compared to the perfect leaf SDR of the JVHW and discrete MI methods as seen in Table~\ref{tb:leaf_sdr_euro}. The poor Gaussian fit seen in the histograms may explain the poor Gaussian based estimation of the algorithm. 


