\subsection{Conclusions on European data sets}

The European data sets offer us new insight into the algorithm performance. The data sets contain samples taken every sixty minutes over the course of a year similar to the IEEE 123 Node data set and unlike the SG1 and SG2 data sets. The data sets all show poor Gaussian approximations for the voltage magnitude data and much better but far from perfect Gaussian approximations for the change in voltage magnitude.

All the data sets except the largest data set, LV semi-urban, show very poor performance when using the discrete mutual information compared to using Gaussian and JVHW mutual information. This is very surprising since the Gaussian approximation is far from a perfect fit. We expect the discrete MI method, provided the discretization scheme is precise enough, to capture the true mutual information better than the rather crude Gaussian fit.  With the same 14 bit discretization the JVHW method performs much better than the discrete MI method for all data sets except the LV semi-urban data set. This suggests the discretization scheme is not to blame for poor discrete MI results. Why the LV semi-urban data set does not show poor discrete MI performance in terms of SDR/leaf-SDR is also perplexing since the voltage magnitude and change in voltage magnitude data as seen in the presented histograms in Figures \ref{fg:LV_semiurban_hist} and \ref{fg:LV_semiurban_deriv_hist} appear very similar to other data sets. 

\section{Discussion}

The MATPOWER data sets show very good results for large over one hundred node networks at a resolution of one sample per hour. The GridLAB-D data sets show very poor performance when downsampled to similar resolutions. The SG2 data sets also show trouble predicting certain nodes even when increasing the length of data considerably. Further work is suggested to ascertain common properties about consistently incorrectly predicted nodes in the SG2 data sets. The European data sets show mixed results at a resolution of one sample per hour. The data sets show decent Gaussian performance but poor JVHW and discrete performance. The poor performance suggests further work is required to delve into the question of whether discretization is causing poor performance in JVHW and discrete MI methods. 

Future work should focus on the further error frequency analysis for SG2 data sets, more use of a mixed Gaussian mutual information based algorithm, creating improved discretization methods, and further analysis into discretization effects for the discrete MI method on European data sets. Lastly, state-of-the-art machine learning methods such as graph neural networks~\cite{wu2020comprehensive}, transformers~\cite{mialon2021graphit}, and neural architecture searches~\cite{zoph2016neural, speckhard2023neural} to find the best architectures have found use in a wide variety of uses in the power grid modeling field~\cite{varbella2024powergraph, mabrouk2022distribution} and may be applicable to these datasets and should be investigated in future work.

\section*{Code availability}

The code was written initially in Matlab.  The main scripts and library of functions used in this work are found on GitHub~\url{https://github.com/speckhard/matlab_grid_estimation}. The algorithm is run with the
\textit{estimate tree main} script. This script takes in a matrix of voltage magnitude data, the true branch list, whether the derivative should be taken, and the number of bits to take. It returns the SDR, the leaf SDR, estimated branches and the mutual information matrix.

The code was then written in python. The repository can be found at \url{https://github.com/speckhard/python_grid_est}. An 8 node IEEE example is shown in an iPython notebook file in the example folder as well as an example main file. The repository can be installed using pip~\url{https://pypi.org/project/grid_top_est/}. 

