\subsection{Output Without Leaking: Semiprime Generator}

We opted not to consider program inputs or outputs as leaked, since such direct information flows can be ruled out in the original Bedrock2 program logic.
This choice also gives us a chance to model cryptographic code in a useful way, where we assume an adversary does not have the computational power to e.g. factor a large secret key that has been output.
%Conservatively, we can consider that the adversary sees those values in full but require that the leakage trace is independent of them.
%
%% Explicit outputs are not treated as leaked, an important feature of our constant-time semantics for nondeterministic programs (even though it may seem counterintuitive).
%% First, the reason this choice is acceptable is that all of our compiler-correctness theorems require I/O traces to be preserved exactly, so any output generated by the compiled program can be precisely characterized at the source level.
%% In particular, the program can be proven against a trace-predicate specification that describes which outputs depend on which inputs similarly to how the compiler's specification describes what the stack-allocation addresses are allowed to depend in.
%
%% Being able to consider outputs not leaked is obviously useful in circumstances where the program's output is still internal to a trustworthy system, say writing a secret key to local storage, and we still want to prove that the key was not leaked.
%% The same semantics feature is also essential even if the output is genuinely external as a workaround to the omission of computational-complexity considerations in leakage semantics.
%% Intuitively, the compiler-generated code is expected to be decently fast, so it should not be able to, for example, compute the secret key from a public key that was leaked per the semantics.
%% Nothing in our compiler-correctness theorems actually captures this restriction: a compiler that generates code to perform exponential-time computations on information leaked by the source program and then leaks the result would still satisfy the same specification.
%% Not treating output as leaked allows public-key-cryptography software to still benefit from our compiler-correctness theorem as long as it can avoid leaking the public key either (to the best of our knowledge, all widely deployed public-key cryptosystems can be efficiently implemented under this constraint\andreser{Really?}).
As an example of this strategy, we proved that the following program that reads two prime numbers as runtime input and outputs their product does not leak the factors:
\begin{verbatim}
Definition semiprime := func! () ~> (p, q) {
  p = getprime(); q = getprime(); n = p * q; output(n) }.
\end{verbatim}
Specifically, we established the following specification: for any \(\mathcal{A}\),
\[
  \exists f. \; \forall m, \ell. \; (\verb|semiprime()|, m, \ell, [], []) \DownarrowA \{([\mathtt{getprime}(p), \;\mathtt{getprime}(q),\; \mathtt{output}(pq)], f())\}.
\]

