\begin{abstract}
QuickSort and the analysis of its expected run time was presented  1962 in a classical paper by C.A.R Hoare. There the run time analysis hinges on a  by now well known recurrence equation for the expected run time, which in turn was justified by referring to ``the law of conditional expectations''. A probability space for the runs of the algorithms was not constructed. Subsequent textbooks treated the recurrence relation as self evident and present it until this day without proof. Here we give an inductive definition of the probability space for the runs of randomized QuickSort and subsequently derive the recurrence equation with a not completely trivial proof.

\smallskip
\noindent \textbf{Keywords.} Randomized algorithm, analysis of algorithms, sorting algorithms, probability spaces.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction and related work}

In~\cite{DBLP:journals/cj/Hoare62}, C.A.R. Hoare introduced the famous QuickSort algorithm. He presented the randomized version of the algorithm,  in which random choices—akin to coin tosses—are used, and derived the equally famous bound $T(n) = O(n \log n)$ for the expected number~$T(n)$ of comparisons. Hoare’s proof relies on the crucial recurrence relation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hoare}
T(n)= n-1+\frac{1}{n}\cdot\sum_{i=1}^n\big( T(i-1)+T(n-i)\big) 
\end{equation}
which he justifies in~\cite{DBLP:journals/cj/Hoare62} by appealing to ``the law of conditional expectations,'' though without providing an explicit construction of a probability space for the algorithm's executions. It is important to note that summing the expectations of multiple random variables formally requires a common underlying probability space on which all of them are defined. The natural approach is to construct such a probability space recursively, by composing probability spaces that model the conditional execution of experiments based on the outcomes of earlier ones.

Although QuickSort and its analysis is classical textbook material, such a construction has, to the best of our knowledge, never made it into textbooks or anywhere else in the literature. The best apparent explanation for this is, that the recurrence equation was presented without proof  in the mother of textbooks on efficient algorithms ~\cite{DBLP:books/aw/AhoHU74}. Thus, in contrast to Hoare, the authors of~\cite{DBLP:books/aw/AhoHU74}, must have considered the equation as self evident,  and outstanding authors of later textbooks (for example~\cite{DBLP:books/daglib/0023376}) continue to present the analysis of randomized QuickSort in this way.

 In this paper we close this gap by presenting the required probability space as part of a mostly self contained
analysis of QuickSort, starting from definitions of elementary probability theory.

  Alternative run time analyses for versions of QuickSort exist. Analysis for randomized version of the algorithm using indicator variables is presented for example in~\cite{DBLP:books/daglib/0012859, DBLP:books/cu/MotwaniR95} and~\cite{DBLP:books/sp/SandersMDD19}. We will discuss the question of the underlying probability spaces for these approaches in Section~\ref{sec:future-work}.


 In the short Section~\ref{subsec:elementary-probability}, we summarize without proof the most basic definitions and facts of elementary probability theory. In the equally short Section~\ref{subsec:conditional-expectation} we survey the law of conditional expectation referenced by Hoare. The novel technical material is in Section~\ref{subsec:conditional-experiments}, where we derive the crucial Lemma~\ref{lem:conditional}, which  analyzes expectations of random variables in probability spaces for the conditional execution of experiments. 

In Section~\ref{sec:expected-runtime-of quicksort}, we present an inductive construction of the probability space of QuickSort runs and conclude the desired recurrence equation from Lemma~\ref{lem:conditional}. We leave the judgment, whether all this is self\nobreakdash-evident for mortals, to the reader. For the sake of completeness we conclude with the derivation of this section run time bound from~\cite{DBLP:books/aw/AhoHU74}. 



