\section{Introduction}


\IEEEPARstart{B}{alanced} distribution networks contributes to reducing voltage violations, line congestion, power loss, etc. Most existing phase balancing and reconfiguration problems are formulated as mixed-integer optimization problems that depend on network topologies~\cite{10098964}. However, these topologies are often unavailable or inaccurate for distribution system operators (DSOs) due to outdated or missing data, such as limited phase labels~\cite{vanin2024phase}. The increasing uncertainty of distributed energy resources (DERs), including household photovoltaic (PV) systems, heating pumps etc., impacts the topology reconfiguration frequency and challenges the identification of low-voltage distribution network (LVDN) topology~\cite{10026490, 10347462, 10475702}. Moreover, existing topology identification methods rely on complete smart meter (SM) datasets, the availability of which is often limited due to privacy concerns~\cite{9696306, costa2022identification}. Synthetic networks and benchmark models in\textcolor{myblue}{\cite{birchfield2016grid,2020Non}} are not practical to represent European LVDNs. Thus, practical topology correction methods for LVDNs are needed for active management and DERs penetration.

The topology identification problem in distribution networks (DNs) could be briefly classified into three categories based on the main issues they are aiming to solve: (1) switch state identification, (2) connection lines and their length identification and (3) phase identification. The switch state identification was modeled as a classification problem in~\cite{9758816}, solved by a trained deep neural network and providing quick estimations under the selected features. To recover the accurate connection lines between transformers and customers, a probabilistic graphical model-based method was developed in~\cite{8456535} by restricting the propagation of SM data error. A power band-based strategy is proposed for dataset selection, aiming to enhance the accuracy of phase identification and transformer-to-meter matching~\cite{10533846}. In~\cite{liu2023hybrid}, a hybrid data-driven approach integrating a partial correlation analysis strategy and a linear regression model is proposed to estimate topology from limited SM data. A similar work in~\cite{wang2023data} established a data-driven model based on the impedance matrix to correct topology and identify the impedance of connection lines  from SM data.

Based on an alternating direction method of multipliers, a robust topology identification method is proposed to estimate the line connection and their parameters using $\mu$-PMUs and SM datasets~\cite{9729419}. Based on incomplete and asynchronous SM data, an optimization-based data-driven approach is constructed to recover the approximate topologies in LVDNs with open datasets~\cite{liu2025topology}. A two-step approach is introduced to identify the topology of LVDNs with multiple meter types, which provide different resolution datasets, mitigating the dependence on the SMs~{\cite{10400804}. For networks with low observability, a distance matrix is introduced in~\cite{10612997} to correct the topology and is also effective in detecting latent nodes positioned between observed nodes along network paths (i.e., intermediate latent nodes rather than endpoints). A physics-informed neural network (PINN) is constructed to estimate the topology by representing the nodes without meters, whose parameters are adjusted by a reinforcement learning-based approach~\cite{10138375}. Although the above methods generate accurate topologies of DNs, the requirements of complete SM data and prior topology information (e.g., the topology candidates) make them infeasible in practice due to data privacy-related concerns. Moreover, optimizing the line resistance and reactance as independent variables may lead to unrealistic parameter estimates, as the properties are inherently interdependent and influenced by physical characteristics of the conductors and environments, such as temperature, humidity.

Related to phase identification, current approaches rely on voltage magnitude data and demand profiles~\cite{7098424}. Since the correlation of time-series voltage magnitude data from the same phase is stronger than that from different phases, correlation analysis is normally used. Given voltage magnitude datasets, spectral clustering and $k$-means clustering approaches are adopted to identify the customer phase label~\cite{8955915}. The mixed integer linear programming model in~\cite{9409966} is constructed to identify the phase labels of single-phase customers by minimizing the gap between transformers' measurement and the estimated data (e.g., power and voltage magnitude) in each phase. An optimization model-based approach was introduced in\textcolor{myblue}{\cite{10098964}} to estimate customer phase labels in LVDNs with limited available recordings. Saliency analysis (SA) was integrated to extract the underlying features aiming to enhance the accuracy of phase label identification~\cite{7604110}} SA and statistical tests are combined in\textcolor{myblue}{\cite{jimenez2020phase}} to guarantee the identification accuracy of the data pairs with weak correlations. Voltage magnitude fluctuations among customers connected to different phases may exhibit similarities due to variations induced by the medium-voltage (MV) network or similar load patterns. To mitigate this effect, a high-pass filter is applied during time-series data pre-processing in the phase identification approach\textcolor{myblue}{\cite{hosseini2020machine}}. Beyond hard classification (i.e., assigning customers a binary label of 0 or 1), soft classification is also considered for phase identification. The method in\textcolor{myblue}{\cite{garcia2023phase}} assigns three probability values to customers. The highest probability indicates the most likely phase label for the user.

