However, the key step in the above approaches is the stepwise subtraction of the identified customers or the users with the highest possibility labels, which ensures the accuracy of the phase labels of the customers who are located far away from the substation. Nevertheless, this key step might induce inaccuracies, since the accuracy of the current step is subject to that of the previous one. To address this issue, based on a modified genetic algorithm, a novel data-driven phase identification approach is introduced to identify the phase labels under incomplete datasets~\cite{jimenez2021new}. Based on multiple linear regression models, \textcolor{myblue}{\cite{cunha2020automated}} presents a hybrid topology identification method to simultaneously identify line parameters and phase connection from raw SM data. Nevertheless, the underlying assumptions of the aforementioned methodologies—such as the presumption of complete dataSETS devoid of SM errors, and the omission of MV network impacts—significantly constrain their practical applicability~\cite{therrien2021assessment}. Fig. \textcolor{myblue}{\ref{Fig1}} presents several underground feeder deployment patterns in the Netherlands~\cite{web1}, which indicates the challenges of LVDN topology identification and correction in practice, e.g., multiple cables under the same street. In addition, some of the above approaches are sensitive to missing data and measurement errors, e.g., SA-based approaches. Furthermore, the user-feeder connection identification issue is rarely considered in the aforementioned papers due to the assumption that one main feeder connects to the transformer.
%  Load profiles based approach might bring more privacy risks to customers \textcolor{myblue}{\cite{9548947}}. To mitigate the impact of electrical distance on the clustering of time series SM data, the power bands-based constraints were incorporated into a two-stage approach to enhance the accuracy of phase identification and transformer-meter \textcolor{myblue}{\cite{10533846}}.

To overcome these limitations, we propose a practical approach for topology correction in LVDNs using incomplete voltage data. Table \ref{table1} highlights the key differences between the proposed approach and existing approaches. By relying solely on voltage magnitude data, this approach preserves privacy and minimizes SM updates, as it releases the need for phase angle measurements from households. The corrected topology provides the foundational network visibility needed to implement load balancing, facilitate DERs integration, and alleviate LV line congestion, enabling active management. The main contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item A data-driven topology correction approach is proposed to correct the missed and outdated recordings in LVDN topologies by three steps: (1) switch state identification, (2) user-feeder correction, and (3) phase label identification. The proposed approach relies solely on voltage magnitude data, reducing privacy risks. Compared to previous methods that depend on complete SM datasets, this approach allows the DSOs to correct the topologies using limited SM data with noise from incomplete household measurements (i.e., not all house SM is accessible).
