power iterations \cite{golub2000eigenvalue}, and highly-parallel spectral
%\jwd{spectral? since Cuppen and Gu+Eisenstat use divide and conquer} 
divide-and-conquer algorithms \cite{Nakatsukasa_Higham_2013,Shah_Hermitian,gemp2020eigengame}. In spite of these more modern options, Jacobi's method has endured, in part because of its simplicity and in part because of its well-documented performance advantages \cite{athi2016real,shiri2019fpga}, particularly the high relative accuracy it can achieve when applied to matrices with small eigenvalues (or small singular values in the SVD case) \cite{DemmelVeselic92}.  In light of its continued relevance, we derive in this paper rigorous complexity bounds for several versions of Jacobi's method, which estimate associated arithmetic and sequential/parallel communication costs. In this context, arithmetic cost concerns the number of floating point operations (flops) performed, while communication refers either to the cost of moving data to/from fast memory of size $M$ (which is assumed to be constant) or between $P$ processors in a network. \\
\indent Absent formal lower bounds, it is natural to conjecture that diagonalizing a symmetric matrix $\M{A}$ is at least as costly, in both arithmetic and communication, as matrix multiplication. In the serial case, our baselines for arithmetic/communication complexity are therefore $\Omega(n^{\omega_0})$ and $\Omega(n^{\omega_0}/M^{\omega_0/2-1})$, respectively, for $2 < \omega_0 \leq 3$.  Here, the specific value of $\omega_0$ is determined by the matrix multiplication algorithm used by Jacobi. $\omega_0 = 3$ corresponds to classical dot-product-based matrix multiplication, for which the communication lower bound was proved by Hong and Kung \cite{HK81}, while $2 < \omega_0 < 3$ designates the wide class of \textit{fast} matrix multiplication algorithms \cite{blaser2013fast}. For these, we focus specifically on Strassen-like options -- i.e., those with recursive structure like that of Strassen's $O(n^{\log_2(7)})$ routine \cite{Strassen69}, which exhibit stability \cite{fastMM} and can be formulated to attain the associated communication lower bound \cite{BDHS12-FastLA,SHS15}. 
%\jwd{Cite Jacob Scott thesis from 2015.} 
This includes the current fastest-known algorithms \cite{faster_MM,fawzi2022discovering}. \\
\indent In the parallel case, we assume that $\M{A}$ is distributed over $P$ processors, each of which has local memory of size $O(n^2/P)$. The analogous matrix multiplication complexity bounds in this setting are $\Omega(n^{\omega_0}/P)$ and $\Omega(n^2/P^{2-\omega_0/2}$) for arithmetic and communication, respectively \cite{BDHLS12-CAPS,BDHS14}. The latter represents the number of words moved between processors in the network and is sometimes called the \textit{bandwidth cost}. The number of messages required by matrix multiplication in this setup, i.e., the \textit{latency cost}, can similarly be bounded from below as $\Omega(P^{\omega_0/2-1})$. \\
%\indent As is typical, our complexity bounds for Jacobi's method are stated in terms of $\omega_0$, which can be thought of either as the true (unknown) complexity of matrix multiplication or as the complexity of the specific routine the algorithm is built upon. 
\indent Our goal throughout is to probe the limits of Jacobi's method to  reach these complexity bounds when built on both classical \textit{and} fast matrix multiplication (hence, some results are stated in terms of $n^3$ while others are in terms of $n^{\omega_0}$). In doing so, we place Jacobi's method at the intersection of two concurrent efforts in numerical linear algebra, which aim to (stably) implement a variety of basic linear algebra computations with arithmetic complexity equal to that of matrix multiplication \cite{DDH07} while also deriving algorithms that minimize associated communication costs \cite{ActaNumerica2014}. We are of course not the first to do this for symmetric eigensolvers, where these complexity considerations go back at least to the late 80s (see for example \cite{Yau_Lu}).

