\indent We now summarize the bounds derived in the remainder of the paper. With the exception of the parallel case, each of these applies to a version of Jacobi's method that is guaranteed to converge (in a way made more precise later), with the assumption that this occurs after at most $O(1)$ \textit{sweeps} over all off-diagonal entries, which is verified experimentally in Section~\ref{section: numerical_ex}. %\footnote{This assumption is verified experimentally in Section~\ref{section: numerical_ex}.}
For a comparison with other solvers for the symmetric eigenproblem, see Table~\ref{tab: complexity_bounds}. To the best of our knowledge, existing Jacobi algorithms either attain complexity or communication lower bounds without a convergence guarantee \cite{SHS15,DrmacVeselic08a,DrmacVeselic08b}, or come with a convergence guarantee at a cost of sub-optimal complexity bounds \cite{Drmac2009}. In this paper, we will attain %\jwd{will attain} 
the best of both worlds, specifically by introducing tree-like recursion into the Jacobi algorithms (See Figure \ref{fig:recursive_structure}). Our contributions can be described as follows: 
%\jwd{Mention both Jacobi with and without convergence guarantees,  since both of interest.}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item In Section~\ref{section: classical} we consider Jacobi's method as originally introduced, which requires $\Theta(n^3)$ arithmetic operations and can be implemented with communication cost $O(n^4/M)$, assuming $\sqrt{M}\leq n < M$. The latter is provably optimal (see Theorem~\ref{thm: classical_lower_bound}).
    \item In Section~\ref{section: block}, we demonstrate that by blocking Jacobi's method we can decrease the arithmetic complexity to $\Theta(n^2b + n^3b^{3-\omega_0})$ for a parameter $0 < b < n/2$ that defines the block size. When $b = \Theta(\sqrt{M})$, this version of Jacobi hits the communication lower bound for $O(n^3)$ matrix multiplication (see Theorem~\ref{thm: blocked_complexity}).
    \item Section~\ref{section: recursive} presents a cache-oblivious, recursive version of the blocked algorithm, which is capable of getting essentially optimal complexity, both in terms of arithmetic operations and sequential communication. In particular, we derive the respective bounds $O(n^{3(1-f)+\omega_0f})$ and $O(\frac{n^{3(1-f)+\omega_0f}}{M^{\omega_0/2-1}})$, where $0 < f < 1$ is the \textit{log block size}. Taking $f$ arbitrarily close to one yields near optimal complexity $O(n^{\omega _0})$ for ``galactically''  large $n$. %,\jwd{making the exponent of $n$ arbitrarily close to $\omega_0$, at the cost of needing ``galactically''  large $n$ to be applicable} (Theorem~\ref{Thm:complexity_recursive_Jacobi}).
    \item In Section~\ref{section: svd} we discuss Jacobi-based SVD algorithms, demonstrating that essentially all of our complexity bounds extend to that setting as well. 
    \item Finally, Section~\ref{section: parallel} considers a parallel implementation of the blocked algorithm from Section~\ref{section: block}. Here, parallelism is achieved by zeroing out suitably independent blocks simultaneously. If done with $P$ processors arranged in a $\sqrt{P} \times \sqrt{P}$ grid (and with classic $O(n^3)$ matrix multiplication), we can achieve a linear speed up in terms of flops -- i.e., computational complexity $O(n^3/P)$.  Moreover, the algorithm is expected to attain the %\jwd{expected?} 
    aforementioned communication bandwidth lower bound $\Omega(n^{2}/\sqrt{P})$ and (nearly) the latency lower bound $\Omega(\sqrt{P})$ (larger by a factor of $\log P$).

\end{enumerate}

Each of Sections~\ref{section: classical}--\ref{section: parallel} is structured as follows:\ after introducing a version of Jacobi's method (or Jacobi SVD), we discuss convergence conditions and prove formal complexity bounds. We follow that in Section~\ref{section: numerical_ex} with a handful of numerical examples. A few important topics are left for future work, specifically the parallel case using fast matrix multiplication and/or more than the minimum memory (analogous to
2.5D matrix multiplication \cite{SD11}).

