\section{Jacobi SVD}\label{section: svd}

As mentioned in Section \ref{sec:intro}, Jacobi's method can be used to compute the SVD of an arbitrary matrix $\M{G} \in {\mathbb R}^{m \times n}$. Indeed, Jacobi-based algorithms are included in the LAPACK SVD drivers as \texttt{xGEJSV} and \texttt{xGESVJ}, with details in 
\cite{DrmacVeselic08a,DrmacVeselic08b}.
%(with implementation details covered in LAPACK working notes 169 \cite{DrmacVeselic08a} and 170 \cite{DrmacVeselic08b}). 
These algorithms are popular for the same reasons as standard Jacobi:\ they can be implemented
efficiently, and retain its accuracy advantages,
i.e. the ability to compute small singular values
to high relative accuracy
\cite{DemmelVeselic92,DGESVD99}. 
% are simple to implement and inherit its performance advantages, where the latter refers to their ability to compute small singular values to high relative accuracy \cite{DemmelVeselic92,DGESVD99}. 
Accordingly, we dedicate this section to discussing Jacobi SVD algorithms and their associated arithmetic/communication complexities. For simplicity, we assume throughout that $m \geq n$.\\
\indent The key insight here is that the SVD of $\M{G}$ can be obtained from a diagonalization of $\M{G}^T\M{G}$, recalling that the right eigenvectors of $\M{G}^T\M{G}$ are right \textit{singular} vectors of $\M{G}$. That is, if $\M{G}^T \M{G} = \M{V} \M{D} \M{V}^T$ for an orthogonal matrix $\M{V}$ and diagonal matrix $\M{D}$, then $\M{G}\M{V} = \M{U} \M{\Sigma}$ for the SVD $\M{G} = \M{U} \M{\Sigma} \M{V}^T$. Intuitively, Jacobi-based SVD algorithms obtain $\M{V}$ by applying Jacobi's method (classical, blocked, or recursive) to $\M{G}^T\M{G}$. In general, this is done without forming the Gram matrix explicitly. Instead, submatrices of $\M{G}^T \M{G}$, which can again be $2 \times 2$ or $2b \times 2b$ and may be handled in a cyclic/random/dynamic order, are computed ``on-the-fly'' from (block) columns of $\M{G}$. The input matrix $\M{G}$ is then transformed to $\M{U}\M{\Sigma}$ by applying the corresponding rotation matrices on the right \textit{only}. Accordingly, this method for computing the SVD is often referred to as ``one-sided Jacobi," which we present here as Algorithm~\ref{alg:Jacobi_SVD}. Note that this routine can be built on top of any version of Jacobi from the preceding sections. 

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{One-Sided Jacobi SVD}
\label{alg:Jacobi_SVD} \begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $\M{G} \in {\mathbb R}^{m \times n}$ with $m \geq n$ and columns partitioned as follows (for $b$ that divides $n$):
\[ \M{G}(:,I) = \M{G}(:, (I-1)b+1:Ib) \; \; \; \text{for} \; \; \; 1 \leq I \leq n/b. \]

\Ensure On output, $\M{G} = \M{U} \M{\Sigma}\M{V}^T$ is an approximate (reduced) singular value decomposition of $\M{G}$, with $\M{U} \in {\mathbb R}^{m \times n}$ and $\M{\Sigma},\M{V} \in {\mathbb R}^{n \times n}$.
\algrule
\Function{$[\M{U},\M{\Sigma},\M{V}]=$
Jacobi\_SVD}{$\M{G}$} \State If singular vectors are requested, $\M{V}=\M{I}_n$
\Repeat \For{all $1 \leq I < J \leq n/b $ in some order,} \State $\M{\hat{A}}= \M{G}(:, [I,J])^T \M{G}(:,[I,J])$ \Comment{$\M{\hat{A}}$ is a $2b \times 2b$ submatrix of $\M{G}^T\M{G}$ formed on the fly} \If{$\M{\hat{A}}$ is far
enough from diagonal} \State $\M{\hat{A}}=\M{\hat{V}}\M{\hat{D}}\M{\hat{V}}^{T}$
is an eigendecomposition of $\M{\hat{A}}$ \Comment{ e.g., obtained from Algorithms~\ref{alg:Jacobi1},\ref{alg:Jacobi2}, or \ref{alg:Jacobi3} } 
\State $\M{\hat{V}}_1 = \M{\hat{V}}(1:b,1:2b)$
\State $[\M{P},\sim,\sim] =$ \textsc{Recursive}\_LUPP$(\M{\hat{V}}_1^T)$
\rlap{\smash{$\left.\begin{array}{@{}c@{}}\\{}\\{}\end{array}\color{black}\right\}%
          \color{black}\begin{tabular}{l}Optional\end{tabular}$}}
\State $\M{\hat{V}} = \M{\hat{V}}\M{P}^T$
\State Multiply block columns $I$ and $J$ of $\M{G}$ by $\M{\hat{V}}$
\State If singular vectors are requested, multiply block columns $I$ and
$J$ of $\M{V}$ by $\M{\hat{V}}$ \EndIf \EndFor \Until{the columns of $\M{G}$ are nearly orthogonal} 
\State $\M{\Sigma} = \text{diag}(||\M{G}(:,1)||_2, \ldots, ||\M{G}(:,n)||_2)$ 
\State If singular vectors are requested, $\M{U} = \M{G}\M{\Sigma}^{\dagger}$ \EndFunction \end{algorithmic} 
\end{algorithm} 

\indent Running Jacobi implicitly on $\M{G}^T\M{G}$ is key to the numerical stability of Algorithm~\ref{alg:Jacobi_SVD}. In particular, forming the Gram matrix $\M{G}^T\M{G}$ squares the condition number (since $\kappa_2(\M{G}^{T}\M{G})=\kappa_2(\M{G})^{2}$),
which can significantly impact the accuracy of the smaller singular values of $\M{G}$ \cite{DrmacVeselic08a,DGESVD99}. Since the popularity of Jacobi-based SVD algorithms is rooted in their ability to compute these singular values to higher precision, avoiding this potential instability is critical. Practical considerations are also a concern, as storing and manipulating an $n \times n$ Gram matrix may present a computational/memory bottleneck in large-scale calculations~\cite{ActaNumerica2014}, though this of course may arise anyways if $b$ is a significant fraction of $n$.\\
\indent The name ``one-sided-Jacobi'' further distinguishes Algortihm~\ref{alg:Jacobi_SVD} from an alternative ``two-sided scaling" approach, in which Jacobi is modified to (1) take a nonsymmetric (and potentially rectangular) input matrix and (2) compute an SVD of each subproblem instead of a diagonalization -- e.g., in line 7 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:Jacobi1}/Algorithm~\ref{alg:Jacobi2} or the base case of Algorithm~\ref{alg:Jacobi3}. Although ``two-sided scaling'' can improve accuracy \cite{matejavs2009accuracy}, we focus on the one-sided version for two reasons. First, it is the more commonly used Jacobi-based SVD algorithm. Second, since the ``two-sided scaling'' approach does not significantly change the structure of Jacobi -- save doubling its memory footprint to allow for left \textit{and} right transformations -- the complexity analysis from the previous sections carries over directly.  \\
\indent Convergence of one-sided Jacobi SVD follows from convergence of classical/blocked Jacobi, provided a cyclic ordering is used on the blocks of $\M{G}^T\M{G}$ and the optional pivoting step in Algorithm~\ref{alg:Jacobi_SVD} is executed. In practice, convergence is accelerated by first computing a QR factorization of $\M{G}$ and running Jacobi implicitly on the Gram matrix of the corresponding R-factor (or its transpose). The high-level outlines is as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Pre-process: $\M{G}\M{P} = \M{Q} \begin{pmatrix} \M{R} \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$
    \item Call Jacobi SVD: $[\M{\hat{V}},\M{\Sigma},\M{\hat{U}}] = \text{Jacobi\_SVD}(\M{R}^T)$
    \item Assemble: $\M{U} = \M{Q}\begin{pmatrix} \M{\hat{U}} \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\M{V} = \M{P}\M{\hat{V}}$ 
\end{enumerate}
The goal here is to both cut down the size of the problem, particularly when the input is tall, and run Jacobi on a matrix that is closer to diagonal than $\M{G}^T\M{G}$. To guarantee the latter, the QR factorization must be computed with column pivoting, which, as discussed at length in Section~\ref{section: block}, will present a computational/communication bottleneck. Further justification for the details of this approach, particularly the choice to run Jacobi SVD on $\M{R}^T$ instead of $\M{R}$, are discussed in \cite{DrmacVeselic08a}. Note also that this heuristic can be applied within Algorithm~\ref{alg:Jacobi_SVD} itself, where we might compute a QR factorization of $\M{G}(:,[I,J])$ and form $\M{\hat{A}}$ from its R-factor, which can be done stably and in a communication-optimal fashion \cite{DemmelGrigoriHoemmen2012}. \\
\indent We are now ready to state arithmetic/communication complexity bounds for Algorithm~\ref{alg:Jacobi_SVD}. We start with a fairly straightforward flop count. The main addition here over standard Jacobi is the cost of forming each $2b \times 2b$ submatrix of $\M{G}^T \M{G}$, which -- like the subsequent block multiplications in lines 11 and 12 -- can be done as $\lceil \frac{m}{2b} \rceil$ square $2b \times 2b$ multiplications. 

%\indent In classical Jacobi for symmetric matrix like Algorithm %\ref{alg:Jacobi1},  sequential rotation zeroes
% out the $(p,q)$ and $(q,p)$ entries of $\bm{A}$ following a dynamic ordering, progressively
% diagonalizing it by appending rotation matrices on both left and right
% sides of $\bm{A}$. By contrast, one-sided Jacobi \cite{DrmacVeselic08a} for the SVD of a general possibly rectangular matrix~$\bm{G}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}$
% applies such rotations only from the right: $\bm{G}[:,[p,q]]\leftarrow\bm{G}[:,[p,q]]\bm{Q}_{(p,q)}$
% which implcitly updates the $2\times2$ submatrix $\bm{G}[[p,q],[p,q]]$.
% Here, we never explicitly form and update $\bm{G}^{T}\bm{G}$; rather,
% each $2\times2$ submatrix of $\bm{G}^{T}\bm{G}$ is computed implicitly
% from the corresponding pair of columns in~$\bm{G}$. Consequently,
% $\bm{G}$ is driven toward $\bm{U}\bm{\Sigma}$ in place, while the
% plane rotations accumulate in an orthogonal factor $\bm{V}$, yielding
% $\bm{G}=\bm{U}\,\bm{\Sigma}\,\bm{V}^{T}$. Forming the entire product
% $\bm{G}^{T}\bm{G}$ explicitly would introduce $O(mn^{2})$ multiplication
% complexity and square any ill-conditioning (since $\kappa(\bm{G}^{T}\bm{G})=\kappa(\bm{G})^{2}$),
% severely affecting the smaller singular values. This phenomenon can
% degrade numerical stability, meaning the computed SVD might lose significant
% accuracy for the smallest singular components~\cite{DrmacVeselic08a,DGESVD99}.
% Furthermore, storing and manipulating an $n\times n$ Gram matrix
% becomes a computational and memory bottleneck in large-scale calculations~\cite{ActaNumerica2014}.

% Instead, by applying rotations directly to blocks or pairs of columns
% of $\bm{G}$ we preserves more numerical precision, particularly for
% ill-conditioned systems, and aligns well with techniques like block
% or recursive Jacobi. Such approaches reduce off-chip communication cost 
% and exploit efficient level-3 BLAS routines.
% As a result, one-sided Jacobi is widely recognized not only for its
