\section{Parallel Jacobi}\label{section: parallel}
Before proceeding with numerical experiments, we consider one final variant of Jacobi's method:\ a parallel version of the blocked algorithm from Section~\ref{section: block}. In this setting, we assume access to $P$ processors arranged in a $\sqrt{P} \times \sqrt{P}$ grid. $b \geq 1$ is again the block size, with the input matrix $\M{A} \in {\mathbb R}^{n \times n}$ partitioned as
\begin{equation}\label{eqn: block_partition}
    \M{A}_{IJ} = \M{A}((I-1)b+1:Ib, (J-1)b+1:Jb)
\end{equation}
for $1 \leq I,J \leq n/b$. For convenience, we further assume that $b$ divides $n$ so that $N = n/b$ is an integer. % Once we solve the blocking strategy, the recursive Jacobi (Algorithm \ref{alg:Jacobi3} alike) can be deployed on each processor to gain the same complexity reduction on the parallel architecture.\\
\indent Since Section~\ref{section: block} covered serial block Jacobi and its convergence properties in depth, we focus here on the parallelization details only. In particular, to simplify the pseudoscode and subsequent analysis, we drop the optional pivoting step (lines 8-10 of Algorithm~\ref{alg:Jacobi2}), even though it is again necessary to guarantee convergence in certain settings. In part, this is a consequence of the reality that partial pivoting is difficult to parallelize (see for example the discussion in \cite[Section 3.3.3]{ActaNumerica2014}). It also reflects the fact that, as we will see, standard row-wise/column-wise cyclic block orderings like those from the previous sections won't be usable here. \\
\indent Parallel versions of Jacobi's method go back decades in the literature \cite{Sameh_1971,Berry_Sameh_1989,Shroff_Schreiber_1989}. The key insight is that multiple rotations can be done simultaneously  provided the corresponding entries/blocks being annihilated are suitably ``independent" -- e.g., they lie in different (block) rows/columns.  We can therefore avoid a serial bottleneck \cite{megiddo1983applying} by ensuring that each parallel step works on independent entries/blocks. Of course, to make the most of efficiency gains we'll also need to make sure that this can be done in a (roughly) load-balanced fashion. For the blocked case specifically, we note the following:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\Mb{A}{IJ}$ and $\Mb{A}{KL}$ are two superdiagonal blocks of
$\M{A}$ (i.e., $I<J$ and $K<L$) that lie in different rows and columns (so $\{I,J\}\cap\{K,L\}=\emptyset$),
then their block Jacobi transformations can be computed independently
and applied in parallel. We call such transformations {\em disjoint}.
Blocks like $\Mb{A}{IK}$, which might require updating by both transformations,
require (matrix) multiplication by one transformation on the left and by the other on the right. Hence, they can be updated in any order. 
\item There are a total of $N(N-1)/2$ pairs $(I,J)$ with $1 \leq I < J \leq N$. We can partition these block indices into $N$ groups, each of size around $N/2$, such that each group contains pairwise disjoint pairs (equivalently, at most one block in each row or column). For example, when $N=8$, we can group the superdiagonal blocks into 8 groups of size 3 or 4 as follows. Here, superdiagonal blocks are labeled by their group number:
\begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}* & 7 & 6 & 5 & 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\
 & * & 5 & 4 & 3 & 2 & 1 & 8\\
 &  & * & 3 & 2 & 1 & 8 & 7\\
 &  &  & * & 1 & 8 & 7 & 6\\
 &  &  &  & * & 7 & 6 & 5\\
 &  &  &  &  & * & 5 & 4\\
 &  &  &  &  &  & * & 3\\
 &  &  &  &  &  &  & *
\end{bmatrix}\label{eqn:disjoint}
\end{equation}
\item Since 
each of these groups consists of one or two diagonals, it is easy to see that their transformations are pairwise disjoint. We illustrate this below with group one, where we now label the four members of the group with different numbers and use them to indicate which blocks of $\M{A}$ their corresponding transformations are applied to. For example, the label ``12" means that transformation 1 is applied on the left and transformation 2 on the right (while the label \textbf{3} means transformation 3 is applied on both the left and right). If eigenvectors are requested, then the transformations are also applied on the right of a cumulative rotation matrix $\M{Q}$.
\begin{equation}\label{eqn: group_one_transformation}
\begin{bmatrix}{\bf 4} & 43 & 42 & 41 & 41 & 42 & 43 & {\bf 4}\\
 & {\bf 3} & 32 & 31 & 31 & 32 & {\bf 3} & 34\\
 &  & {\bf 2} & 21 & 21 & {\bf 2} & 23 & 24\\
 &  &  & {\bf 1} & {\bf 1} & 12 & 13 & 14\\
 &  &  &  & {\bf 1} & 12 & 13 & 14\\
 &  &  &  &  & {\bf 2} & 23 & 24\\
 &  &  &  &  &  & {\bf 3} & 34\\
 &  &  &  &  &  &  & {\bf 4}
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
\item If the blocks are all assigned to different processors
(arranged in an $8 \times 8$ processor grid in this case) then the work represented by \eqref{eqn: group_one_transformation} is load
balanced. This is approximately true for the other groups in (\ref{eqn:disjoint}), even though they do not necessarily update all block rows/columns from the left and right. For example, group two does not update blocks (4,4), (4,8) and (8,8) (missing updates are indicated by ``\_''): 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn: alt_transformations}
\begin{bmatrix}{\bf 3} & 32 & 31 & 3\_ & 31 & 32 & {\bf 3} & 3\_\\
 & {\bf 2} & 21 & 2\_ & 21 & {\bf 2} & 23 & 2\_\\
 &  & {\bf 1} & 1\_ & {\bf 1} & 12 & 13 & 1\_\\
 &  &  & \_\_ & \_1 & \_2 & \_3 & \_\_\\
 &  &  &  & {\bf 1} & 12 & 13 & 1\_\\
 &  &  &  &  & {\bf 2} & 23 & 2\_\\
 &  &  &  &  &  & {\bf 3} & 3\_\\
 &  &  &  &  &  &  & \_\_
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
These cases, while not perfectly load balanced, are good enough for our purposes. Hence, we take $b=n/\sqrt{P}$ throughout, which, again, is assumed to be an integer for simplicity.
\item As suggested by the above examples, we can technically store $\M{A}$ using only half of the processors in the grid by keeping only its upper triangle. Nevertheless, we need all $P$ processors to store/update the eigenvector matrix $\M{Q}$ (assuming it is blocked in the same way as $\M{A}$) as it is nonsymmetric in general. %\hl{I feel like this point may cause a bit confusion between Jacobi and Jacobi SVD. } 
For this reason, and since it would change our subsequent complexity bounds by only a constant factor, we work with the full matrix $\M{A}$ over the entire processor grid.
\end{enumerate}

