\section{Conclusions and Open Problems}
% \indent In this paper, we have demonstrated how to solve the symmetric eigenproblem (and compute the SVD) with essentially optimal arithmetic and communication complexities via Jacobi's method. Our bounds apply in a variety of settings, ranging from serial to parallel and with/without fast matrix multiplication. 
This paper presented a detailed analysis of the arithmetic and communication complexity of Jacobi’s method for the symmetric eigenvalue problem and singular value decomposition. We examined classical, blocked, and recursive formulations of the algorithm with the aim of pushing its complexity towards that of matrix multiplication,
both in terms of computational efficiency and data movement. \\
\indent Our study began with  classical, scalar Jacobi, for which we established a tight communication lower bound that exceeds that of matrix multiplication, even under optimal data access patterns. We then considered a blocked version of the algorithm, which leverages data locality to attain the communication lower bound of classical $O(n^3)$ matrix multiplication (when the block size is tuned to memory constraints). To ensure convergence in this setting, we proposed a lightweight approach based on fast, recursive PLU, which offers a practical alternative to expensive QR-based pivoting schemes. \\
\indent Building on these ideas, we then developed a recursive, cache-oblivious version of Jacobi’s method, which is guaranteed to converge while achieving near-optimal complexity (in both computation and communication). This formulation, alongside the classical and blocked algorithms, was also shown to extend naturally to a one-sided Jacobi SVD algorithm. In the parallel setting, we designed a blocked Jacobi algorithm that achieves linear speedup in floating-point operations and nearly optimal communication bounds when distributed over a two-dimensional processor grid, establishing (recursive) Jacobi as a competitive parallel eigensolver in terms of scalability. Finally,  our theoretical developments were supported by extensive numerical experiments. % by recursively applying block Jacobi and terminating at a fixed error and block-size threshold. Through a careful choice of recursion depth and block size, we demonstrated that it can match the arithmetic and communication efficiency, while retaining the stability properties of Jacobi’s method. This formulation was also shown to extend naturally to one-sided Jacobi SVD algorithms.

%Theoretical developments were supported by extensive numerical experiments. We evaluated the role of recursion depth, base-case accuracy, and pivoting strategies on convergence. These results highlight the robustness and efficiency of recursive Jacobi when practical safeguards are applied, such as early stopping and adaptive thresholding. Taken together, our findings establish that Jacobi’s method is not only long valued for its numerical stability but can also be systematically restructured to meet the demands of modern architectures, offering a compelling blend of accuracy and scalability.

\indent Extending the recursive formulation to the parallel setting, particularly with fast matrix multiplication and/or with more than $O(n^2/P)$ memory per processor, remains an open problem. Investigating mixed-precision \cite{Gao2025mixed, higham2025computing} %or \hl{low-rank adaptations} 
could also yield further gains in performance and resource efficiency. Finally, a detailed floating-point error analysis of the recursive method and its application to generalized and Hermitian eigenproblems would expand the practical reach of Jacobi-based techniques (and their generalizations \cite{detherage2025unified,kolda2009tensor}).



