        (\verb|line 7|) If the transition's target state $s$ already belongs to some procedure (i.e., the whole invocation entry transition belongs to some procedure), no changes have to be made to the register operations. The algorithm simply copies the transition to the corresponding procedure states specified by $Q_{proc}$. This is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:call_mapping}, where the algorithm creates, from invocation transitions $\transition{q_1}{a,*/*}q_3$, $\transition{q_1}{f,0/1}{q_3}$, and $q_1\crightarrow{$\{g, 1 / 1\}$}q_3$, new procedure transitions $q_1\crightarrow{$\{a, */*\}$}q_{3,4}$, $q_1\crightarrow{$\{f, 0 / 1\}$}q_{3,4}$, and $q_1 \crightarrow{$\{g, 1 / 1\}$} q_{3,4}$.
