\begin{abstract}

    %Voice Activity Detection (VAD) plays a vital role in speech processing by distinguishing speech from non-speech segments. Traditional approaches rely on handcrafted acoustic features like Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCC) and energy thresholds, while modern deep learning methods utilize self-supervised learning (SSL) models such as Wav2Vec2. In this work, we introduce FusionVAD, a novel method that enhances speech and non-speech classification by combining MFCC with embeddings from state-of-the-art (SOTA) SSL models using simple feature concatenation. We also conduct a comprehensive comparative study of leading SSL models. Our approach is evaluated on three open-source datasets—AMI Corpus, Callhome, and Voxconverse—using Detection Error Rate as the evaluation metric. Experimental results show that FusionVAD outperforms cross-attention-based methods, offering superior accuracy and robustness. The concatenation approach preserves the complementary strengths of spectral and contextual features without introducing unnecessary dependencies, making it computationally efficient and effective. Our findings suggest that direct feature fusion provides a more reliable VAD solution, particularly in noisy environments. %FusionVAD offers a promising direction for improving real-time speech applications such as automatic speech recognition, speaker diarization, and telecommunication systems.

    %Voice Activity Detection (VAD) is a crucial speech processing task that differentiates speech from non-speech segments. Traditional approaches rely on handcrafted features like Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCC), while modern deep learning methods leverage pre-trained models (PTM) such as wav2vec 2.0. Although attention mechanisms have been widely adopted in modern speech processing, our study reveals that attention is not always essential for tasks like VAD. We demonstrate that a simple feature concatenation or addition of MFCC and state-of-the-art (SOTA) PTMs outperforms cross-attention-based methods in speech and non-speech classification. Additionally, we conduct a comparative analysis of SOTA PTMs for VAD. Through experiments on open-source datasets- including AMI, Callhome, and VoxConverse, we show that direct feature fusion retains essential spectral and contextual information without introducing redundant dependencies, leading to improved accuracy and computational efficiency. These findings highlights the importance of task-specific model design. Our results suggest that lightweight, non-attention-based models can achieve robust VAD performance. %, making them suitable for real-time speech applications such as ASR and speaker diarization.

    Voice Activity Detection (VAD) plays a key role in speech processing, often utilizing hand-crafted or neural features. This study examines the effectiveness of Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs) and pre-trained model (PTM) features, including wav2vec 2.0, HuBERT, WavLM, UniSpeech, MMS, and Whisper. We propose FusionVAD, a unified framework that combines both feature types using three fusion strategies: concatenation, addition, and cross-attention (CA). Experimental results reveal that simple fusion techniques, particularly addition, outperform CA in both accuracy and efficiency. Fusion-based models consistently surpass single-feature models, highlighting the complementary nature of MFCCs and PTM features. Notably, our best-performing fusion model exceeds the state-of-the-art Pyannote across multiple datasets, achieving an absolute average improvement of 2.04\%. These results confirm that simple feature fusion enhances VAD robustness while maintaining computational efficiency.

\end{abstract}


