\section{Methodology}

\subsection{MFCC vs PTM Features}

%Pre-trained model based features are shown to be effective for many speech tasks including VAD. PTM feature extractors are trained to capture dependency information in speech and can provide contextual embedding with the help of self-attention. This kind of dependency information can benefit many speech processing tasks and can help perform well in noisy environment. Whereas traditional hand crafted features such as spectrogram and MFCCs can provide static time-frequency information. For detecting speech segments in an audio signal, time-frequency based information can be sufficient under clean environment. The problem  arises under noisy condition where unexpected noisy frequency components interfering speech frequencies making these features difficult to distinguish between speech and non-speech. In this scenarios, PTM features can provide robust representations as they trained on data consisting of various acoustic conditions and noises and can benefit VAD. So, it is important to understand the prominence of PTM features over traditional hand crafted features. This understanding can help in improving the performance of VAD if both PTM and hand-crafted features found to capture complimentary information.

Pre-trained model based features have proven effective for various speech tasks, including VAD. These models leverage self-attention to capture long-range dependencies and generate contextual representations, which are particularly beneficial in noisy environments. In contrast, traditional hand-crafted features like spectrograms and MFCCs offer static time-frequency representations. While such information may be sufficient for speech detection in clean conditions, it becomes less effective in noisy settings where overlapping frequency components obscure speech cues. In these cases, PTM features provide more robust representations, as they are trained on diverse acoustic conditions and noise types. This robustness makes them valuable for improving VAD performance under challenging conditions. Understanding the strengths of PTM features relative to traditional features is essential, especially if they are found to encode complementary information. Combining both types of features could potentially enhance overall VAD performance by leveraging the contextual awareness of PTMs and the fine-grained spectral detail of hand-crafted features.

