So far all macros were treated equally when extracting dataflow relationships, without considering their individual characteristics, especially area. Larger macros typically occupy more chip area and are more prone to causing congestion or other placement violations. To address this, we propose a feedback model that incorporates macro characteristics into the loss function during the Simulated Annealing process, refining connectivity and placement based on macro size. Specifically, the model adjusts the weights of two-hop connections in a cascading manner, where the influence of second-hop cell clusters is weighted differently based on their connection to source macros. Additionally, macro area within the cluster is factored into the placement optimization, as larger macros are more likely to impact floorplan congestion, while smaller ones allow greater placement flexibility. The final placement is further refined based on these weighted adjustments, producing an optimized floorplan. While other features, such as the number of cell clusters, could also influence placement, they are omitted here to maintain variable uniformity. The following sections detail the methodology and implementation.
%The extraction of connectivity relations contributes to the performance of macro cluster and cell cluster placement in floorplan, but is not sufficiently guaranteed for the variability and validity between each macro. Therefore, we propose a feedback model of macro characteristics to adjust the weight of the loss function in the simulated annealing SA process for the last connectivity characteristic of each macro, and at the same time, the area of the macro in the macro cluster is taken into account to further optimize the whole congestion of the placement. Specifically, we first adjust the weights of the 2-hop connections in a cascading nature, so that the cell clusters of the 2 hops create a differential effect on the macros of the sources. Then, we consider the effect of macro area in the macro cluster on the overall placement. Where larger macros tend to affect floorplan congestion, smaller area designs are more suitable for flexible placement. Eventually, we will further optimize the macro placement results based on these two analyses to get new floorplan results. Note that there are still other features, such as the number of cell clusters, which we will not discuss more here for the sake of controlling the uniformity of the variables. We give the detail introduction in the following part.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Macro Characteristic Backward-feedback Model}} 
As discussed in Section \ref{cell-cell-flow}, the 2-hop {\tt macro-cell-cell} connectivity relationship is reflected by $w_2 * WL_{m-c-c}$ in the loss function in Equation \ref{equation_final_loss}. Here, only the strength of the connectivity between cell clusters was considered. While this approach links macros to two-hop cell clusters, it only captures the connection strength between one-hop and two-hop cell clusters without distinguishing the unique influence of different source macros. As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:finetuning1}(a), the connection strength between the B1 cell cluster and the two macros (M1 and M2) remains identical, despite the differences in the source macros’ characteristics. Since the goal is to improve macro placement, it is essential to account for the varying connectivity strength between the same two-hop cell cluster and different macros. To address this, we propose coupling the weights of one-hop macro-cell clusters with the weights of two-hop cell-cell clusters. This allows the feedback from the source macro’s characteristics to propagate through the connectivity network, creating differentiated connection strengths for different source macros, as shown in Equation \ref{finetuning_0}. This coupling reflects both the local connection strength and the global impact of macro characteristics, enhancing the accuracy of the placement optimization process.
% In Section \ref{cell-cell-flow}, only the strength of the connectivity relationship between cell clusters and cell clusters was considered, using $loss = w_2 * WL_{m-c-c -c}$ to characterize the 2-hop macro-cell-cell connectivity relationship. Although this connection method connects macros to 2-hop cell clusters, it can only reflect the connection tightness between 1 hop-cell clusters and 2 hop cell clusters, and cannot distinguish the difference between different source macros. As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:finetuning1}(a), B1 cell cluster as a 2-hop cell cluster connected to the indicated macro clusters of M1 and M2 has no difference in the connection strength of the weight response. Since our main goal is to optimize macro placement, we need to seek a method that reflects the different connectivity between the same 2-hop cell cluster and different macros. In order to be able to reflect the uniqueness that each source macro cluster has, we believe that the weights connected to 2-hop cell clusters should be dependent on the preorder state, i.e., the connection weights of 1-hop macro-cell clusters are coupled with the weights of 2-hop cell-cell clusters as shown in Equation \ref{finetuning_0}.
\begin{gather}
    loss_{m-c-c} = w_1 * w_2 * WL_{m-c-c}
     \label{finetuning_0}   
\end{gather}


\subsubsection{\textbf{Feature-driven Placement Optimization}} 
%除了多跳之间的连接强度之外，我们也考虑了macro的面积对macro摆放位置及floorplan中congestion的影响。按照后端工程师的经验，较大的macro应尽量贴边摆放给cell cluster留出更多的摆放空间，而较小的macro则应该优先选择最有利于提升摆放性能的位置。因此，对于不同面积的macro也应该纳入到我们最终loss function的考虑中。如图8所示，连向同一个cell cluster的不同面积的macro cluster的权重w与area成反比。
Beyond multi-hop connection strength, macro area significantly impacts floorplan congestion and overall placement quality. Based on back-end design experience, larger macros should be placed near the chip boundary to reserve more space for cell clusters, while smaller macros should be positioned closer to cell clusters to enhance placement performance. To integrate this consideration, macro sizes are incorporated into the final loss function. As illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:finetuning1}(b), the larger macro cluster M1 is strategically positioned at the chip boundary, while the smaller macro cluster M2 is placed closer to the cell cluster, influenced by the cell cluster’s pull. This area-based placement strategy ensures optimal utilization of available space and reduces routing congestion, thereby improving overall design efficiency.

%In addition to the connection strength between multiple hops, we also consider the impact of macro area on macro placement and congestion in floorplan. According to the experience of back-end engineers, larger macros should be placed as close as possible to the edge to leave more space for cell clusters, while smaller macros should be prioritized to choose the location that is most conducive to improving the placement performance. Therefore, macros of different sizes should also be considered in the final loss function. As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:finetuning1}(b), the larger macro cluster M1 is placed to the chip boundary, while the smaller macro cluster M2 is placed close to the cell cluster based on the pull of the cell cluster.

\subsubsection{\textbf{New Loss Function for Optimizing the Macro Placement}}
%针对上述的两部分优化，我们将针对2hop的前序影响及每个macro的面积考虑在内，提出了新的用于模拟退火的loss function，如下所示。loss = w * w1 * WL。
Based on the fine-tuning processes described in the prior sections, we introduce a refined loss function that incorporates both the connection weights of 2-hop macro cluster-cell cluster-cell cluster connections and macro area into the simulated annealing process. In Equation \ref{area}, the macro area $A_i$ is normalized to the interval [1,2] based on the maximum and minimum macro areas in the design, yielding $A_i'$. The loss function for the 2-hop connection is then updated by adjusting the weights and incorporating the area constraints, as shown in Equation \ref{finetuning}.
\begin{gather}
A_i' = 1 + \frac{(A_i - A_{\min})}{A_{\max} - A_{\min}} 
    \label{area}   
\vspace{-5pt}    
\end{gather}

\begin{gather}
    loss_{m-c-c} = \frac{\sqrt{w_1 * w_2}}{A_i'} * WL_{m-c-c}
     \label{finetuning}   
\end{gather}

%For the above two-part optimization, we consider the effect of connection weights for 2-hop macro cluster-cell cluster-cell cluster connection and the area of each macro on the final placement effect, and propose a new loss function for simulated annealing as follows.
In Equation \ref{area}, the macro area $A_i$ is normalized to the interval [1,2] based on the maximum and minimum macro areas in the design, resulting in $A_i'$. The loss function for the 2-hop connection is then updated by incorporating the revised weights and area constraints, as shown in Equation \ref{finetuning}.
By leveraging the final SA loss function, we obtain SA-based macro placement results, which serve as the foundation for further macro orientation optimization through flipping.
%In Equation \ref{area}, we normalize the area by regularizing $A_i$ to the interval [1,2] based on the maximum and minimum area of macro in a design to obtain $A_i'$. We then update the loss function of the 2-hop connection based on the update of the weights as well as the area constraints to obtain Equation \ref{finetuning}.

\subsection{Macro Orientation Optimization}
Most automatic floorplanning tools focus on macro placement, often neglecting macro flipping, which can effectively reduce wirelength and improve timing and other design metrics \cite{incredflip}. In advanced technology nodes, macro orientation is typically restricted to 0 and 180 degrees to prevent layer misalignment. If rotation is necessary, it is limited to flipping along the x and y axes, effectively capping adjustments at 180 degrees. We define four flipping modes: no flipping (N), x-axis flipping (FN), y-axis flipping (FS), and both x and y-axis flipping (S).

In the fine-tuning process, we begin from a selected point based on the dataflow connection to the I/O pins. The flipping order of subsequent macros is determined by their topological relationships within the dataflow while respecting dataflow-based order constraints. To model the macro flipping problem accurately, we perform dataflow vectorization and decomposition, using superimposed vectors to guide the flipping direction.

% In the fine-tuning process, we start from a selected point based on the dataflow connection to the I/O pins, the flipping order of subsequent macros is determined by considering their topological relationships in the dataflow, while adhering to the constraints of the dataflow-based order. 
% Most automatic floorplanning tools focus on macro placement, often overlooking macro flipping, which can effectively reduce wirelength and optimize timing and other metrics.
% In advanced technology nodes, it is common practice to keep the orientation of two macros between 0 and 180 degrees to prevent layer disorientation. If rotation is necessary, it is restricted to flipping along the X and Y axes, effectively limiting adjustments to 180 degrees.
% To model the macro flipping problem accurately, we perform dataflow vectorization and decomposition and use superimposed vectors to guide flipping direction. 

Since dataflow inherently encodes the connection information of a design through its directional and weighted nature, making it well-suited for vector-based representation. By mapping dataflow into a vector space ($V_T$), we identify three distinct vector types relevant to macro flipping:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $V_{mm}$ — vectors between macros
    \item $V_{mc}$ — vectors from a macro to cell clusters
    \item $V_{mcc}$ — vectors from a macro to cell clusters at a further hop.
\end{itemize}
As discussed in Section \ref{cell-cell-flow}, we limit the analysis to two hops from macros, as experiments show that standard cells beyond this range have minimal influence on macro positioning. We then analyze the vector projections along the x- and y-dimensions, denoted as $x{V_T}$ and $y{V_T}$, respectively.
% dataflow inherently carries connection information of a design, characterized by its directional and weighted nature, making it ideally suited for representation as vectors. 
% By extracting dataflow within a vector space ($V_T$) in a design, we can identify three distinct vector types relevant to macro flipping. 
% %By extracting dataflow within a vector space ($V_T$) in a design, we can identify three distinct vector types relevant to macro flipping. 
% These include ($V_{mm}$), representing vectors from one macro to another, ($V_{mc}$), denoting vectors from a macro to cells clusters, and ($V_{mcc}$), which are vectors from a macro to cell clusters at a further hop. Our focus is limited to two hops from macros, as our experiments have shown that standard cells beyond this range show less influence on macro positioning. Consequently, we analyze the projections in the vector space along the x-dimension and y-dimension, denoted as $x\{V_T\}$ and $y\{V_T\}$.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Dataflow Decomposition of $V_{mm}$}}
\label{sec:vmm}
A key step in decomposing vectorized dataflow is identifying the vector’s starting and ending points. In the proposed method, the origin for decomposition coordinates is set at the midpoint of all pin positions on the target macro. By focusing on pin positions, the macro flipping problem is effectively reduced to accurately positioning these pins within each macro.
%A critical step in decomposing vectorized dataflow involves identifying the vector's starting and ending points. In the proposed method, we use the midpoint of all pin positions on the target macro as the origin for decomposition coordinates. By focusing on the pin positions, we transform the challenge of macro flipping into one of accurately positioning these pins within each macro. 
In many designs, physical connections between macros are rarely strictly horizontal or vertical and often involve multiple connections, necessitating dataflow decomposition, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:macro flipping}(a). During decomposition, vertical orientation guides upward or downward flipping, while horizontal orientation guides left or right flipping. The vector direction between macros is determined by the position of the target macro’s pin relative to the connected macro’s pin, with its magnitude calculated as the product of the dataflow exchange’s bitwidth and the physical distance between pin centers (see Equation \ref{eq: define vector}, where $m2$ is the center macro). For macros with multiple out-degree connections, the additive property of vectors allows the overlay of decomposed vectors along the same axis, enabling the calculation of dataflow magnitude in all directions relative to the coordinate axes, as defined in Equation \ref{eq : x + y}, where $n$ is the number of connections for the macro of interest.
% In many designs, the physical connections between macros are neither strictly horizontal nor vertical and often involve multiple connections, requiring dataflow decomposition, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:macro flipping}(a). During decomposition, the vertical orientation guides upward or downward flipping, while the horizontal orientation guides left or right flipping. The vector direction between macros is determined by the position of the target macro’s pin relative to the connected macro’s pin, and its magnitude is calculated as the product of the dataflow exchange’s bitwidth and the physical distance between pin centers (see Equation \ref{eq: define vector}, where $m2$ is center macro). For macros with multiple out-degree connections, the additive property of vectors allows us to overlay decomposed vectors along the same axis, enabling the calculation of dataflow magnitude in all directions relative to the coordinate axes, as defined in Equation \ref{eq : x + y}, where $n$ is the number of connections for the macro of interest.
\begin{equation}
V_{mm} = bitwidth * ((x_{m_1}, y_{m_1}) - (x_{m_2}, y_{m_2})) 
    \label{eq: define vector}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
    \begin{gathered}
        x \{V_{mm}\} = x\{V_{mm_1}\} + x\{V_{mm_2}\} + ... + x\{V_{mm_n}\} \\
        y \{V_{mm}\} = y\{V_{mm_1}\} + y\{V_{mm_2}\} + ... + y\{V_{mm_n}\}
    \end{gathered}
\label{eq : x + y}
\end{equation}


